.TH OTPME-UNIT 1 "2025-12-29" "OTPme 0.3.0a120" "OTPme Manual"
.SH NAME
otpme-unit \- manage OTPme organizational units
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B otpme-unit
.I command
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIunit\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B otpme-unit
manages organizational units in the OTPme system. Units provide a hierarchical structure for organizing objects such as users and groups.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Unit Management
.TP
.B add \fIunit\fR
Add a new unit.
.TP
.B del \fIunit\fR
Delete a unit.
.TP
.B show \fR[\fIunit\fR]
Display unit information.
.TP
.B list \fR[\fIregex\fR]
List units, optionally filtered by regex pattern.
.TP
.B enable \fIunit\fR
Enable a disabled unit.
.TP
.B disable \fIunit\fR
Disable a unit without deleting it.
.TP
.B rename \fIunit\fR \fInew_name\fR
Rename a unit.
.TP
.B move \fR[\fB\-m\fR] [\fB\-k\fR] [\fB\-o\fR \fIobject_types\fR] [\fB\-\-keep\-acls\fR] [\fB\-\-skip\-ca\fR] \fIunit\fR \fInew_unit\fR
Move unit to a different unit. Use \fB\-m\fR to merge objects into destination, \fB\-k\fR to keep source unit. A CA cannot be moved: with \fB\-\-skip\-ca\fR everything else is moved and the source unit is kept. This is not the same as \fB\-f\fR, which only skips the confirmation.
.TP
.B touch \fIunit\fR
Re-index the object to fix potential index problems.
.SS Unit Configuration
.TP
.B config \fR[\fB\-d\fR] [\fB\-a\fR] \fIunit\fR \fIparameter\fR \fR[\fIvalue\fR]
Set a configuration parameter. Use \fB\-d\fR to delete (reset to default) or \fB\-a\fR to append the value to a list-typed parameter.
.TP
.B show_config \fIunit\fR [\fIparameter\fR]
Show unit config parameters.
.TP
.B get_config \fIunit\fR \fIparameter\fR
Show the value of a single configuration parameter.
.TP
.B description \fIunit\fR \fR[\fIdescription\fR]
Set unit description.
.TP
.B info \fR[\fB\-\-language\fR \fILANG\fR] \fIunit\fR \fR[\fIinfo\fR]
Set free-form info text. If \fIinfo\fR is omitted, the current info text is
opened in the editor specified by the \fBEDITOR\fR environment variable.
.TP
.B dump_info \fR[\fB\-\-language\fR \fILANG\fR] \fIunit\fR
Dump the info text to stdout.
.SS Password Members
The config parameter
.B password_store_members
names who gets the keys of the passwords below the unit, see
.BR otpme\-pass (1).
The members count only when a user signed them.
.TP
.B sign_password_members \fIunit\fR
Sign the members with your sign key.
The signature covers the members and the users they stand for now.
.br
It first shows what changed since your last signature, and asks.
The global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
When nothing changed, it says so and signs nothing.
.br
Whether a signature counts is decided by each host: only a signer of type
\fBpassword\fR set up there makes it count, see
.BR otpme\-tool (1).
.br
Needs the ACL \fBset:config:password_store_members\fR.
.TP
.B show_password_members \fIunit\fR
Show the members, the users they stand for, and each signature.
A signature is up to date, or out of date with the users it lacks
(\fB+\fR) and the ones it still names (\fB\-\fR).
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview:config:password_store_members\fR.
.TP
.B unsign_password_members \fR[\fB\-\-signer\fR \fIuser\fR] \fIunit\fR
Remove your signature.
With \fB\-\-signer\fR the one of that user.
For a user that is gone, give the UUID that \fBshow_password_members\fR
shows.
.br
It asks first; the global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
The members and the other signatures stay.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBset:config:password_store_members\fR.
.SS Object Changelog
Every change to the object writes an entry: who made it, when, and what was done.
A change can carry a comment of its own, through the global option \fB\-\-changelog\fR.
.PP
An \fIentry_id\fR is the number the listing shows in front of an entry, counted from 0.
.PP
Only the comment of an entry can be changed or removed.
What was done and by whom stays as it is.
.PP
A command that changes something asks first; the global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.TP
.B changelog \fIunit\fR
Show the changelog of the unit.
Needs the ACL \fBview:changelog\fR.
.TP
.B edit_changelog \fIunit\fR \fIentry_id\fR \fIcomment\fR
Set the comment of that entry, or replace the one it has.
Needs the ACL \fBedit:changelog\fR.
.TP
.B del_changelog \fIunit\fR \fIentry_id\fR
Remove the comment of that entry.
The entry itself stays, and an entry without a comment is refused.
Needs the ACL \fBedit:changelog\fR.
.TP
.B clear_changelog \fIunit\fR
Remove every entry of the changelog.
.br
This needs more than the ACL \fBclear:changelog\fR: the config parameter
.B allow_changelog_clearing
has to allow it (see \fBotpme\fR(7)).
It is true by default, but where no value is found at all, clearing is refused.
OTPme asks the token you are signed in with first, then the object.
.SS Policy Management
A \fIpolicy\fR is the name of a policy, not a path.
It is looked for in the site of the unit.
Which policies there are, and what each of them does, is in
.BR otpme\-policy (1).
.PP
Attaching and detaching ask before they change anything; the global option \fB\-f\fR skips the question.
.TP
.B add_policy \fIunit\fR \fIpolicy\fR
Attach a policy to the unit.
.br
A policy has a list of object types it is for; one that does not name \fBunit\fR is refused.
So is a second policy of a type that allows only one per object.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBassign:policy\fR.
.TP
.B remove_policy \fIunit\fR \fIpolicy\fR
Remove a policy from the unit.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBremove:policy\fR, and that one verbatim: a plain \fBremove\fR does not cover it.
Detaching a policy takes away what it enforces, so it must not be cheaper than attaching one.
.TP
.B list_policies \fR[\fB\-\-return\-type\fR \fITYPE\fR] [\fB\-\-policy\-types\fR \fIt1,t2\fR] \fIunit\fR
List the policies attached to the unit.
Use \fB\-\-return\-type\fR to select the attribute returned (\fBname\fR, \fBread_oid\fR, \fBfull_oid\fR, \fBuuid\fR).
Use \fB\-\-policy\-types\fR to list only policies of the given types; these are policy types, not policy names.
.br
Needs the ACL \fBview:policies\fR.
.SS ACL Management
.TP
.B add_acl \fR[\fB\-r\fR] [\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-\-objects\fR \fItypes\fR] [\fB\-\-procs\fR \fInum\fR] \fIunit\fR \fIowner_type\fR \fIowner\fR \fIacl\fR
Add an access control entry. Use \fB\-r\fR for recursive, \fB\-a\fR to apply default ACLs to existing objects.
.RS
.PP
A recursive run walks every object of the unit and of the units below it. Where there are many, the objects are shared out over several processes.
\fB\-\-procs\fR says how many. Without it a quarter of the cores of the node, which leaves the rest for the daemons that go on serving logins while this runs.
.RE
.TP
.B del_acl \fR[\fB\-r\fR] [\fB\-a\fR] [\fB\-\-objects\fR \fItypes\fR] [\fB\-\-procs\fR \fInum\fR] \fIunit\fR \fIacl\fR
Remove an access control entry. \fB\-\-procs\fR as above.
.TP
.B list_acls \fIunit\fR
Display all ACLs for the unit.
.TP
.B enable_acl_inheritance \fIunit\fR
Enable ACL inheritance from parent objects.
.TP
.B disable_acl_inheritance \fIunit\fR
Disable ACL inheritance.
.SS Extension and Attribute Management
.TP
.B add_extension \fIunit\fR \fIextension\fR
Add an extension to the unit.
.TP
.B remove_extension \fIunit\fR \fIextension\fR
Remove an extension from the unit.
.TP
.B add_attribute \fIunit\fR \fIattribute\fR=\fIvalue\fR
Add an LDAP attribute to the unit.
.TP
.B modify_attribute \fIunit\fR \fIattribute\fR \fIold_value\fR \fInew_value\fR
Modify an LDAP attribute of the unit.
.TP
.B del_attribute \fIunit\fR \fIattribute\fR=\fIvalue\fR
Delete an LDAP attribute from the unit.
.TP
.B add_object_class \fIunit\fR \fIclass\fR
Add an LDAP object class to the unit.
.TP
.B del_object_class \fIunit\fR \fIclass\fR
Delete an LDAP object class from the unit.
.TP
.B show_ldif \fR[\fB\-a\fR \fIattributes\fR] \fIunit\fR
Show LDIF representation of the unit. Use \fB\-a\fR to show only specific attributes.
.SS Import/Export
.TP
.B export \fR[\fB\-\-password\fR \fIpassword\fR] \fIunit\fR
Export unit config to stdout.
.TP
.B remove_orphans \fR[\fB\-r\fR] \fIunit\fR
Remove orphan UUIDs. Use \fB\-r\fR for recursive.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Display Options
.TP
.B \-a
Show all units.
.TP
.B \-z \fISIZE\fR
Limit output size.
.TP
.B \-\-fields \fIFIELD1,FIELD2\fR
Display only specified fields.
.TP
.B \-\-policy\-limit \fIN\fR
Limit number of policies shown.
.TP
.B \-\-limit \fIN\fR
Limit number of items shown per object.
.TP
.B \-\-sort\-by \fIFIELD\fR
Sort output by field.
.TP
.B \-\-reverse
Reverse sort order.
.TP
.B \-\-raw
Output without headers/borders.
.TP
.B \-\-csv
Output as CSV.
.TP
.B \-\-csv\-sep \fISEP\fR
CSV separator character.
.TP
.B \-\-attribute \fIATTR\fR
Of \fBlist\fR: match the regex against this attribute instead of the name.
.SS Move Options
.TP
.B \-m
Merge objects from source unit into destination unit.
.TP
.B \-k
Keep source unit.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-acls
Keep object ACLs.
.TP
.B \-o \fIuser,group,...\fR
Move only given object types.
.SS General Options
.TP
.B \-r
Recursive (for ACL and remove_orphans operations).
.TP
.B \-a
Apply default ACLs to existing objects (for ACL operations).
.TP
.B \-\-objects \fItype1,type2\fR
Limit ACL operations to specific object types.
.TP
.B \-\-password \fIPASS\fR
Password for encrypting exports.
.PP
Global options are available for all commands. See
.BR otpme (1)
for details.
.SH CONFIG PARAMETERS
Configuration parameters can be set with the \fBconfig\fR command and displayed with \fBshow_config\fR.
For a complete description of all available parameters and their applicable object types, see
.BR otpme (7).
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B otpme-unit add it
Create a new unit
.TP
.B otpme-unit add it/admins
Create a sub-unit
.TP
.B otpme-unit move \-m old_unit new_unit
Merge objects from old_unit into new_unit
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/otpme/
OTPme data directory
